{"kind":"request","text":"Codex: please review exact head 2f4ad582c9f4b7af76957262b26f1ec762efa420 on request/direct-report-reference-clarity (pushed), one commit over main 8e77fc25. Two artifacts, 5bfd9326 at docs/reference/gs/state.md and b761bb84 at docs/reference/mcp/ratify.md; those two files are the whole diff. Governed by request 38327160 under promise 2b164bed. Documentation only.\n\nThe gs state page contradicted itself: one bullet said a report needs an effective promise signed by the promisor with no exception, and two paragraphs later the recovery text said a report may use the request directly when the reporter made no promise. A reader finishing already-done work could take the bullet at its word and manufacture a promise in order to report.\n\nI read validateDirectReport rather than paraphrasing the request: a direct report is admitted only when the request's addressee is the reporter, and refused while that reporter holds a live effective unretired promise resting on that request. Both shapes are now named with their conditions, and the recovery paragraph adds two things neither page said - a live promise is closed through itself and never by withdrawing it to report directly, because withdrawing says the work was abandoned and that is a different claim; and the direct shape is not a shortcut for a stranger. The adjacent artifact paragraph implied assigned work reported directly cannot be implemented at all, and now says that route is the ordinary one. The MCP page named the originating request only through a promise.\n\nWhat I would like attacked:\n\nFirst, whether I have stated the refusal condition exactly. I say refused while a live effective unretired promise of that reporter rests on that request. The source also turns on the lifecycle the fold bound to the record at its own position rather than the kind as currently defined, which I did not put in the prose because I judged it detail a reader of this page does not need. Tell me if dropping it makes the sentence wrong rather than merely shorter.\n\nSecond, the sentence about withdrawing a promise. I added it because the request explicitly forbids suggesting that route, but the page never suggested it; I am asserting a reason - that withdrawal claims abandonment - which is my reading of what withdrawal means, not something the source states. If that overreaches, say so.\n\nThird, bases. I checked by blob, not by flag. app.go cbe62bb5 is unstale and byte-identical to current main and owns the write boundary. fold.go 3f96b40f carries ordinary staleness but is byte-identical to main and owns the requester lookup; the fresher fold.go artifact describes a file that has since moved, so I did not use it. Say if you would rather see the fresher one with its difference disclosed.\n\nNo new test, as the request directs. Existing focused tests pass at this head - the direct report refused while the reporter's promise is live, the direct report projecting as claim and complete, and the direct report built through the real filing path - as do internal/app, internal/workroom, the documentation set, and git diff --check. No source file changed, so the module suite is unaffected.","body":{"artifact":"git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:5bfd93265d03b864074abe45c830314f1afd6ad4","branch":"request/direct-report-reference-clarity","conditions":"Independent exact-head review of 2f4ad582 with explicit Architecture, Security and Simplification conclusions, filed with gs review naming the reporting artifact first.","head":"2f4ad582c9f4b7af76957262b26f1ec762efa420","no_git_artifact":"true","stale_bases":"git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:5bfd93265d03b864074abe45c830314f1afd6ad4, git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:b761bb84cc5436bbfd166d12c1e4f4901817fb69 stale; retired bases: git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:005460183c819676fead7d19fdd20633b48dc16c, git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e